1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,363
MissingLink is a transport improvement planner.

2
00:00:02,363 --> 00:00:07,483
Two services can use the same stop, yet their timetables fail to connect.

3
00:00:07,483 --> 00:00:12,208
A passenger arriving just after a departure can face a long wait.

4
00:00:12,208 --> 00:00:17,328
MissingLink finds these repeated problems and shows which timetable changes are worth investigating.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,781
The overview uses real National Transport Authority timetable data for Dublin.

6
00:00:23,781 --> 00:00:28,511
It identifies 1,308 poor connection patterns across 595 locations.

7
00:00:28,511 --> 00:00:34,292
At 591 locations, at least one tested shift improves a connection.

8
00:00:34,292 --> 00:00:38,496
These are scheduled transfer opportunities, not passenger counts.

9
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:41,913
The map shows where poor transfers occur.

10
00:00:41,913 --> 00:00:48,113
Five priority places highlight the strongest opportunities, with the current wait, a suggested departure shift, and

11
00:00:48,113 --> 00:00:49,276
the simulated wait.

12
00:00:49,276 --> 00:00:52,376
This gives a planner a clear starting point.

13
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:57,268
The full ranking keeps every affected location available, grouped by stop.

14
00:00:57,268 --> 00:00:59,921
Each row shows that place's strongest opportunity.

15
00:00:59,921 --> 00:01:05,984
Search and peak filters narrow the results, so a planner can compare places without inspecting connections

16
00:01:05,984 --> 00:01:07,500
one at a time.

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,348
Let's explore DCU Collins Avenue.

18
00:01:10,348 --> 00:01:13,786
Both poor connections at this stop appear together.

19
00:01:13,786 --> 00:01:18,941
We will investigate the evening transfer from route 220 to route 104.

20
00:01:18,941 --> 00:01:21,949
Its current median wait is 64 minutes.

21
00:01:21,949 --> 00:01:25,816
The proposed change delays connecting departures by three minutes.

22
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:29,582
The optional simulator starts with the current timetable.

23
00:01:29,582 --> 00:01:33,050
At zero shift, the simulated median remains 64 minutes.

24
00:01:33,050 --> 00:01:39,215
The analysis includes a two minute boarding allowance, so a departure at the arrival time is

25
00:01:39,215 --> 00:01:40,756
not a usable connection.

26
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:47,019
With a three minute shift, the median wait becomes two minutes for this connection.

27
00:01:47,019 --> 00:01:48,990
All three scheduled arrivals improve.

28
00:01:48,990 --> 00:01:55,297
But the stop wide check exposes a tradeoff: across other incoming routes, 16 arrival opportunities worsen.

29
00:01:55,297 --> 00:02:00,028
This is a candidate to investigate, not an automatic recommendation to implement.

30
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:03,601
Show evidence reveals the actual timetable comparison.

31
00:02:03,601 --> 00:02:10,233
For the first arrival, at sixteen fourteen, the next usable departure changes from seventeen eighteen to

32
00:02:10,233 --> 00:02:11,062
sixteen seventeen.

33
00:02:11,062 --> 00:02:13,963
Waiting falls from 64 to three minutes.

34
00:02:13,963 --> 00:02:16,864
The other two waits become two minutes.

35
00:02:16,864 --> 00:02:22,252
The sample is small, and operational constraints and effects elsewhere still need validation.

36
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,424
MissingLink turns timetable data into a practical planning workflow: find the problem, compare opportunities, test a

37
00:02:29,424 --> 00:02:31,432
change, and check the evidence.

38
00:02:31,432 --> 00:02:37,856
The goal is to help transport teams investigate better connections, with the benefits and tradeoffs visible.